---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
import { links, REPO } from '../data/site';
import {
  processModel,
  crateMap,
  vaultMemory,
  daemon,
  channels,
  mcpGateway,
  herdr,
  governance,
  releasePipeline,
  topologies,
} from '../data/diagrams';

const toc = [
  ['process', 'Process model'],
  ['crates', 'Crate map'],
  ['vault', 'Vault and memory'],
  ['daemon', 'Daemon'],
  ['relays', 'Chat relays'],
  ['web', 'Web PWA'],
  ['mcp', 'MCP server'],
  ['herdr', 'Herdr sessions'],
  ['tasks', 'Tasks'],
  ['governance', 'Governance and sandbox'],
  ['updates', 'Signed updates'],
  ['topologies', 'Deployment'],
  ['source', 'Source map'],
];

const sourceMap = [
  ['CLI binary', 'crates/hq-cli/src/main.rs'],
  ['Agent session', 'crates/hq-agent/src/session/mod.rs'],
  ['Context engine', 'crates/hq-agent/src/context/engine.rs'],
  ['Daemon scheduler', 'crates/hq-cli/src/commands/start/daemon/mod.rs'],
  ['MCP server', 'crates/hq-mcp/src/server.rs'],
  ['Herdr driver', 'crates/hq-tools/src/herdr/'],
  ['Harness sessions', 'crates/hq-tools/src/harness_session/'],
  ['Bash sandbox', 'crates/hq-agent/src/bash_sandbox.rs'],
  ['Governance rules', 'crates/hq-agent/src/governance/'],
  ['Updater library', 'crates/hq-update/'],
  ['Web auth and origin guard', 'crates/hq-web/src/auth.rs'],
  ['Native tasks', 'crates/hq-db/src/tasks.rs'],
];
---

<Layout
  title="Architecture"
  description="How Agent HQ is built: one Rust binary, a markdown and SQLite vault, a daemon, chat relays, a web PWA, an MCP gateway, supervised coding-agent sessions, a bash sandbox and a signed updater."
  path="/architecture/"
>
  <section class="band">
    <div class="container section">
      <span class="eyebrow">For engineers</span>
      <h1>Architecture</h1>
      <p class="lede prose">
        HQ is one Rust binary around one folder of markdown. This page walks through each part in the
        order you would meet it, with the code paths to read. Everything here comes from the repository
        documentation. Where a document and the code could drift, the page points at the file.
      </p>
      <nav aria-label="On this page">
        <ul class="toc">
          {toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container">
    <section class="doc-section" id="process" aria-labelledby="process-h">
      <h2 id="process-h">Process model and the single binary</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The <code>hq</code> binary is built from the <code>hq-cli</code> crate. A release build is
          about 40 MB with LTO and symbol stripping. The same file runs the terminal chat, the
          daemon, the Discord and Telegram relays, the web server and the MCP server. You pick the
          role with a subcommand.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>hq start all</code> starts the long-lived components and <code>hq ps</code> lists the
          managed processes. An MCP client does not need any of that: it launches
          <code>hq mcp-serve</code> over stdio itself. <code>hq chat</code> runs the agent in
          process, so the terminal works with nothing else running.
        </p>
        <p>
          Configuration lives in <code>~/.hq/config.yaml</code>, or the file named by
          <code>HQ_CONFIG_PATH</code>. Any key can also come from an <code>HQ_</code> environment
          variable, with <code>__</code> for nesting. The file and its backups are written owner-only.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={processModel} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="crates" aria-labelledby="crates-h">
      <h2 id="crates-h">Crate map</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The workspace is Rust edition 2024 with 14 crates. Foundations hold types, storage and the
          LLM clients. The agent crate holds the session loop, sub-agents, governance and the context engine.
          Services wrap those for a transport. <code>hq-cli</code> ties them into the binary.
        </p>
        <p>
          The stack, in short: Tokio for async, Axum and tower-http for the web server, rusqlite
          (bundled, WAL mode) for storage, rmcp for MCP, serenity for Discord, teloxide for Telegram,
          clap for the CLI and figment for configuration. LLM calls go through async-openai with a
          swappable base URL, which is how OpenRouter, Ollama and other OpenAI-compatible backends
          plug in.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={crateMap} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="vault" aria-labelledby="vault-h">
      <h2 id="vault-h">The vault and the memory layers</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The vault is a folder, <code>.vault/</code>, and it is the single source of truth. Notes,
          memory, skills and conversation threads are markdown with YAML frontmatter. One SQLite file,
          <code>.vault/_data/vault.db</code> in WAL mode, holds the indexes: FTS5 full-text search,
          embedding vectors, link graphs and the registries the daemon and relays need. Delete the
          database and the notes are still there.
        </p>
        <p>
          Embeddings come from OpenRouter when a key is set, and from Ollama
          (<code>nomic-embed-text</code> by default) when it is not. Memory, consolidation and skill
          review call the router under the aliases <code>dream</code>, <code>bulk</code> and
          <code>ingest</code>. Point those at a local model with a <code>backends:</code> block and
          they stay on your machine.
        </p>
        <h3>Memory</h3>
        <p>
          The <code>hq-memory</code> crate has an ingester, a consolidator, a querier, a forgetter and
          an entity graph. The daemon runs consolidation, embedding, note tagging and forgetting on
          its memory tier, so memory is something the vault does for itself over time, not a log that
          grows forever.
        </p>
        <h3>Context frames</h3>
        <p>
          Each turn, the context engine builds a token-budgeted frame from five layers: system, the
          user message, memory, thread and injections. Surplus tokens cascade between layers, with the
          thread getting 50 percent, injections 35 percent and memory 15 percent. Private tags are
          stripped from the memory layer, and older thread messages are compacted.
        </p>
        <h3>Who may see what</h3>
        <p>
          Notes and tasks can carry a label: private to named people, shared, public, or unmarked,
          which means owner only. A shared channel discloses only what every verified participant may
          see, and an unresolved identity is denied by default.
          <a href={links.sec.identity}>The design note</a> lists what is not covered yet.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={vaultMemory} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="daemon" aria-labelledby="daemon-h">
      <h2 id="daemon-h">The daemon and its scheduled tasks</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The daemon is a plain interval scheduler. It ticks every 5 seconds and runs whichever tasks
          are due, one after another, each with a timeout derived from its interval tier. A missed
          task is detected on the next start. After every tick it writes
          <code>DAEMON-STATUS.md</code> at the vault root, so you can read what ran with
          <code>cat</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fast tier handles approvals, harness-session supervision, the heartbeat, value-bus
          delivery and the email poll. The memory tier handles consolidation, embeddings, note tagging
          and forgetting. The maintenance tier handles vault health, thread rotation, clean-up, a disk
          watchdog and SQLite vacuum. Beside the scheduler, an agent worker triages inbound events and
          email, and a machine profile records which CLIs the host has.
        </p>
        <p>
          Recurring prompts are not daemon tasks. They are <code>/watch</code> rows, covered with the
          relays below.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={daemon} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="relays" aria-labelledby="relays-h">
      <h2 id="relays-h">Chat relays</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Discord and Telegram are built into <code>hq-relay</code> as feature-gated bridges behind
          one unified bot. They share commands and watch firing, so behavior matches. Send
          <code>!cancel</code> in either and the agent stops at the next tool boundary and replies
          with a summary of turns and files touched. A heartbeat message shows the turn count and the
          tool currently running.
        </p>
        <p>
          Chat bridges are gated to an owner. Run <code>hq pair</code>, then send
          <code>/pair &lt;code&gt;</code> in a private Telegram chat or <code>!pair &lt;code&gt;</code>
          in a Discord DM. Earlier versions let the first sender become the owner. That was changed
          because it hands the agent to whoever messages first.
        </p>
        <h3>Turns that outlive the chat</h3>
        <p>
          A turn has an ack window, 270 seconds by default. If the turn runs longer, the chat gets an
          immediate acknowledgement and the session detaches into a persisted
          <code>background_turns</code> table, then delivers its result when it finishes. A reconciler
          runs at daemon start and every 6 hours. It marks rows that were running when something died
          as interrupted, offers a <code>resume &lt;id&gt;</code>, and retires rows older than five
          days.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>/watch &lt;minutes&gt; [for &lt;N&gt;h] &lt;prompt&gt;</code> stores a durable
          recurring turn in the same table, so watches survive restarts. A reply containing
          <code>WATCH_DONE</code> delivers once and stops the watch.
        </p>
        <h3>Session limits</h3>
        <p>
          Sessions have no turn-count ceiling. They end on completion, cancellation, an optional USD
          budget cap, or a wall-clock limit that defaults to five hours.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={channels} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="web" aria-labelledby="web-h">
      <h2 id="web-h">The web PWA</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The Control Center is a React 19 app on TanStack Start and Vite, built to a static
          single-page bundle. <code>hq start all</code> serves it from <code>:5678</code> along with
          the REST API and the chat socket, from <code>web/dist</code> next to the vault or the
          directory in <code>web_static_dir</code>. There is no separate web server process.
        </p>
        <p>
          It streams the agent live, browses and searches the vault, shows daemon status, runs the
          tasks views, lists and drives sessions, renders DOCX, XLSX and PDF, and takes push
          notifications. It installs on a phone like any PWA.
        </p>
        <h3>Auth in the browser</h3>
        <p>
          The server binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default. With <code>web_auth_token</code> set,
          the token is accepted only as a Bearer header, never in a URL. A browser cannot set headers
          on a WebSocket, so the app asks <code>POST /api/ws-ticket</code> for a ticket that works
          once, lasts 30 seconds and opens only <code>/ws</code>. An origin guard refuses requests from
          origins that are not loopback, not the server's own and not listed in
          <code>web_allowed_origins</code>, which also stops DNS rebinding. The response CSP has no
          <code>unsafe-eval</code> and no script <code>unsafe-inline</code>, and remote images are
          blocked on purpose, because an image URL is a way to send data out of a rendered note.
          Details: <a href={links.sec.webAuth}>web and MCP auth</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="mcp" aria-labelledby="mcp-h">
      <h2 id="mcp-h">The MCP server and the two-tool gateway</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          HQ is an MCP server so the agents you already use can reach your vault, tasks and sessions.
          A client always sees exactly two tools. <code>hq_discover</code> searches the registry by
          keyword and returns matching tools with their descriptions. <code>hq_call</code> runs a tool
          by name with JSON input. The registry behind them holds about a hundred tools, and a client
          that calls <code>hq_discover</code> at runtime always sees the live list.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>hq mcp install --target claude-code</code> writes the client config for Claude Code,
          Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, Copilot, OpenCode, Antigravity or a project file, and
          <code>hq mcp doctor</code> checks the connection.
        </p>
        <h3>Keys and scopes</h3>
        <p>
          Over HTTP, <code>/mcp</code> refuses every call unless a key is configured. There are three
          scopes. A full key sees everything. A read-only key sees an allowlist. A handoff key gets that
          read-only set, minus session logs, plus filing tasks, starting sessions and asking questions
          in read-only mode, and nothing else. The gateway sets
          the scope itself, so a client cannot claim a wider one. Treat the handoff key as the power to
          start a coding agent anywhere you allow it, and set <code>herdr.handoff_cwd_allow</code> to
          bound where.
        </p>
        <h3>Both directions</h3>
        <p>
          HQ also bridges remote MCP servers. Each <code>remote_mcp:</code> entry becomes a
          <code>&lt;name&gt;_discover</code> and <code>&lt;name&gt;_call</code> pair in the registry,
          kept out of unattended sessions by default. From outside, <code>hq_ask</code> lets an MCP
          client put a question to HQ's own chat agent, in an ordinary web thread you can open and
          continue (<a href={links.mcpAsk}>docs</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={mcpGateway} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="herdr" aria-labelledby="herdr-h">
      <h2 id="herdr-h">Herdr and supervised coding-agent sessions</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <a href={links.herdr}>Herdr</a> owns the terminals. It recognises which coding agent sits in
          a pane and reports its state: idle, working, blocked on a dialog, or done. HQ drives it
          through the <code>herdr</code> CLI. Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Pi, OpenCode, Copilot CLI,
          Kimi, Qwen and Antigravity are supported harnesses, and you can define your own profiles.
          This replaced an earlier tmux runner that could only guess state from screen text.
        </p>
        <h3>Hosts</h3>
        <p>
          A host is a machine running Herdr. <code>local</code> is the machine HQ runs on. Any other
          host is reached over ssh with a dedicated key that is pinned to one source address and to a
          forced command, a small gate script that accepts the <code>herdr</code> CLI's arguments as
          JSON on stdin and refuses <code>server</code>, <code>session</code>, <code>machine</code>
          and remote flags. That is how HQ on a VPS can run and watch agents on a laptop, whenever the
          laptop is on and reachable. Connections are reused between calls, and a write is never
          retried because the gate may already have run it.
        </p>
        <h3>Spawning and watching</h3>
        <p>
          <code>harness_session_spawn</code> starts an agent and returns a session id. Send, wait,
          logs, status, stop and resume sit beside it, and <code>hq sessions</code> mirrors them in the
          CLI. A supervisor in the daemon polls every host once a minute. It alerts once when a
          session blocks and once when an agent finishes a turn. A host that cannot be reached counts
          as unknown, never as exited: a sleeping laptop leaves its sessions running and they are
          watched again when it answers.
        </p>
        <p>
          HQ does not answer dialogs for you. Claude Code's workspace-trust prompt defaults to "No,
          exit", so pressing Enter would quit it. A blocked launch is reported with the screen, and you
          answer with explicit keys.
        </p>
        <h3>Sessions that work on a task</h3>
        <p>
          A session can be linked to a task. Each event leaves a task comment, and the task moves from
          in progress to ready for review or blocked as the session finishes or exits. HQ never marks a
          task complete, because an agent saying it is done is not verification. Comments never quote
          the pane, since pane text is untrusted input.
        </p>
        <h3>Handoff</h3>
        <p>
          <code>harness_session_handoff</code> lets an outside client, such as another agent over MCP,
          start tracked work in one call. It files or reuses a task by an idempotency key, starts one
          linked session with the acceptance criteria as its definition of done, and opens a chat
          thread that owns the session. It is idempotent: the same key returns the same task, and a
          task that already has a running session gets no second one.
        </p>
        <h3>Watching and driving from a web chat</h3>
        <p>
          A chat can watch a session. Watching posts short updates and never quotes the screen.
          Driving lets HQ act as the session's project manager: read the session, send the next
          instruction, approve a permission prompt or resume an exit, toward the linked task's goal. It
          asks you instead when a step is destructive, touches credentials, deploys or spends money.
          A new watch starts with Drive on only when the task gives it a usable goal, and
          observation-only otherwise. A turn that has read untrusted content starts new watches with
          Drive off, and a tool can never turn it back on once you switched it off. Mechanical limits cut it off
          without relying on the model: an instruction budget per session, a cap on key presses, a
          no-progress guard, and a cap on sessions driven at once. Each one switches Drive off and
          says why. The Sessions page shows every session with its task, host, goal and live state.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={herdr} />
      <p class="prose muted">
        Full reference, including setup and troubleshooting:
        <a href={links.herdrHarness}>docs/HERDR_HARNESS.md</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="tasks" aria-labelledby="tasks-h">
      <h2 id="tasks-h">Tasks</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Tasks are native, with no external tracker to mirror. The model is spaces, then initiatives,
          then tasks, then comments, with sub-tasks, start dates and soft dependencies. One shared
          write surface in <code>hq-db</code> backs the MCP tools (<code>task_list</code>,
          <code>task_create</code>, <code>task_update</code>, <code>task_comment_add</code> and
          friends), the REST API with its WebSocket broadcast, and the web UI with list, board and
          timeline views. Tasks have no CLI command.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tagging a task routes it to an agent mailbox synchronously. A person's tasks live in a
          private space that only that person's turns can read. See
          <a href={links.nativeTasks}>the design note</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="governance" aria-labelledby="governance-h">
      <h2 id="governance-h">Governance and the bash sandbox</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The agent's <code>bash</code> tool runs model-written shell commands, so HQ does not rely on
          the model refusing. A governance layer denies dangerous effects mechanically, before a tool
          runs, and it applies in permissive modes too.
        </p>
        <p>
          Credential files such as <code>~/.ssh</code>, cloud credentials and the HQ config are
          unreachable always. A session becomes tainted once a tool returns untrusted content: web
          fetch and search, mail, converted documents, other agents' mailboxes, session logs, pane
          reads, vault reads and any remote MCP result. Taint is shared with every sub-agent the
          session spawns, so delegating cannot launder it. After taint, project secrets such as
          <code>.env</code> files and outbound network from bash (<code>curl</code>,
          <code>ssh</code>, <code>nc</code> and similar) are denied.
        </p>
        <p>
          For bash, three layers follow. Text checks catch honest mistakes and cheap tricks, but about
          100 known obfuscation shapes are pinned in tests and some still get through, so they are
          speed bumps. An environment allowlist starts the child with an empty environment and a short
          list of variables. HQ's own keys are refused even if you list them. Then the OS sandbox:
          bubblewrap on Linux, <code>sandbox-exec</code> on macOS. On Linux it gives a read-only root,
          a fresh <code>/proc</code> in a new pid namespace, writable binds only for home, the working
          directory, temp and the vault, and optionally no network. Config, env files and private
          SSH keys are masked.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>governance.bash.sandbox</code> takes <code>off</code>, <code>best_effort</code> or
          <code>required</code>. After 0.9.0 the default is <code>required</code>: if no sandbox backend
          works, every bash command is refused and <code>hq doctor</code> says so.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={governance} />
      <p class="prose muted">
        What this does not cover is on the <a href="/security/">security page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="updates" aria-labelledby="updates-h">
      <h2 id="updates-h">The signed release and pull-updater pipeline</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A server instance pulls its updates. Nothing on the build side holds a credential for any
          instance, and instances hold only a public key.
        </p>
        <h3>Build side</h3>
        <p>
          Every merge to <code>main</code> runs tests, builds on an older Ubuntu image so the binary
          needs only an older glibc, and publishes a signed prerelease named like
          <code>0.9.1-main.6</code>. The job that runs repository build code never sees the signing
          key: a separate sign job does. Each release carries the Linux x86_64 binary archive, the web
          bundle, <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, a <code>manifest.json</code> and its
          <code>.minisig</code>. Channel pointers (<code>channel-main</code>,
          <code>channel-stable</code>) are small signed JSON files that say which release a channel
          currently means. Promoting to stable signs a new pointer for an existing release. Nothing is
          rebuilt.
        </p>
        <h3>Instance side</h3>
        <ol class="facts">
          <li>
            A systemd timer fires every 10 minutes with a short random delay and runs
            <code>hq update --apply</code> under an exclusive lock.
          </li>
          <li>
            It verifies the pointer signature, checks that the manifest URL lives under the configured
            repository, then verifies the manifest signature and its SHA-256, the schema and the
            minimum updater version.
          </li>
          <li>
            Equal or lower versions do nothing. A pointer with a lower sequence number than one already
            accepted is refused as a replay.
          </li>
          <li>
            It downloads with hard size caps, verifies each artifact's SHA-256 against the signed
            manifest, and unpacks with a strict tar reader that rejects links, devices, absolute and
            parent paths and duplicates.
          </li>
          <li>
            It snapshots the vault database with <code>VACUUM INTO</code>, as the service user, never
            as root.
          </li>
          <li>
            It runs the staged binary as the service user with <code>--version</code> and requires the
            exact build. This is the only downloaded code that runs before the swap.
          </li>
          <li>
            It swaps the binary and web files by rename, keeping the last three binaries and one old
            web tree, restarts the service and polls <code>/health</code> until it reports the new git
            sha.
          </li>
          <li>
            On success it runs <code>hq install --upgrade</code>. On failure it rolls back, restores
            the database only if migrations ran, blocks that version for 24 hours and sends an alert.
            A crash mid-update is recovered from a journal on the next run.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Signatures are minisign (Ed25519), verified in process. The key is read from a root-owned
          file or compiled in, and cannot be overridden by an environment variable. Key rotation is a
          manual step. A compromised signing key and a stale-pointer freeze attack are listed as not
          mitigated. Read <a href={links.updateSystem}>docs/UPDATE_SYSTEM.md</a> for the contract.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={releasePipeline} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="topologies" aria-labelledby="topologies-h">
      <h2 id="topologies-h">Deployment topologies</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Laptop only.</strong> Run <code>hq start all</code>. The web server binds to loopback.
          Set <code>web_auth_token</code> on a shared machine. This is the default and needs no network
          setup.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>VPS plus Tailscale.</strong> Provision a Debian or Ubuntu box with
          <code>deploy/setup-vps.sh</code>, install the signed updater with
          <code>deploy/install.sh</code>, and keep HQ on loopback. Caddy bridges to
          <code>tailscale serve</code>, which terminates HTTPS on your tailnet only, so the UI never
          touches the public internet. List every origin you serve the UI from in
          <code>web_allowed_origins</code>. HQ on the VPS can then drive Herdr on a laptop over the
          gated ssh key.
        </p>
        <p>
          The only endpoint that may ever be public is <code>/mcp</code>, through a proxy that exposes
          <code>/mcp</code> and <code>/health</code> and nothing else, with an API key set.
          <code>/ws</code> and <code>/api</code> must never be public. A host runs one deploy path
          only: the pull updater, or a manual build, not both. Steps are on the
          <a href="/install/">install page</a> and in <a href={links.deployReadme}>deploy/README.md</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Diagram d={topologies} />
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="source" aria-labelledby="source-h">
      <h2 id="source-h">Source map</h2>
      <p class="prose">Where to start reading, relative to <a href={REPO}>the repository</a>.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Source map table">
        <table>
          <caption>Entry points</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">Path</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              sourceMap.map(([area, path]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{area}</th>
                  <td><a href={`${REPO}/${path.endsWith('/') ? 'tree' : 'blob'}/main/${path}`}><code>{path}</code></a></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
